<Base title="Stacks · ITC 120" description="Stacks (last in, first out): push, pop, and peek, traces, real-life uses, and a stack in Java using an array." course={{ code: course.code, href: base + course.href }} toc>
  <header class="wrap lesson-hero flow">
    <p class="crumbs"><a href={base + course.href}>ITC 120</a> › Prelim › Stacks</p>
    <p class="kicker">Linear data structures · Lesson 3 of 4</p>
    <h1>Stacks</h1>
    <KindChips />
    <p class="lede">The newest item leaves first, like the top plate on a pile.</p>
    <div class="goals">
      <p class="note-label">By the end, you can:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>explain the last-in, first-out (LIFO) rule and name places where it fits;</li>
        <li>trace <code>push</code>, <code>pop</code>, and <code>peek</code> in a table, writing the stack from bottom to top and the value each call returns;</li>
        <li>say what each <code>ArrayStack</code> method changes in <code>data</code> and <code>top</code>;</li>
        <li>explain underflow and overflow, and how the code guards against them.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <p class="muted small"><b>You need:</b> the <a href={base + '/itc120/arrays/'}>Arrays</a> lesson. The <a href={base + '/itc120/linked-lists/'}>Linked lists</a> lesson helps for one optional part.</p>
  </header>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="idea">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 1 · The big idea</p>
      <h2>A pile with one open end</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>After a <i>handaan</i>, the clean plates go on one pile. You put each washed plate on the top, and when someone needs a plate, they take the top one. The plate that went in <b>last</b> comes out <b>first</b>.</p>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={platesPic()} /><figcaption>Plates go on and come off at the top only. The bottom plate went in first and will come out last.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="def">
        <p class="note-label">Stack</p>
        <p>A <b>stack</b> is a linear data structure (its items form one line) where items are added and removed at <b>one end only</b>, called the <b>top</b>. It follows the <b>LIFO</b> rule: <b>last in, first out</b>. The item added most recently is the first one removed.</p>
      </div>
      <p>Here is the definition as a picture. Four items went in, one after another:</p>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig"><Fragment set:html={defSvg} /><figcaption>The items form <b>one line</b>, from the bottom to the top. Only the <b>top end is open</b>, so every push and every pop happens there. Read the two sides: A went in first but comes out last; D went in last and comes out first. That is LIFO.</figcaption></figure>
      <p>A stack is not a new way of <i>storing</i> data. It is a <b>rule</b> placed on storage you already know, such as an array: "only touch the top." The cells stay the same; the rule only changes which cells you may use:</p>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig"><Fragment set:html={stkRulePic()} /><figcaption>Top: a plain array lets you use any index. Bottom: the same five cells with the stack rule. 4, 9, 2 are the stack, 2 is the top item, and the next push goes into cell 3. The cells under the top are still there, but you may not reach in and take or change 9 or 4.</figcaption></figure>
      <p><b>Why limit yourself?</b> Many tasks only ever need the newest item (you will see some below). With the rule, adding or removing never shifts other items, as inserting into the middle of an array does, so push and pop take the same few steps however big the stack gets. And no part of the program can mix up the order by reaching into the middle.</p>
      <p>Here are the words that come with a stack:</p>
      <div class="scroll-x">
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>Word</th><th>Meaning</th><th>With the plates</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><b>top</b></td><td>the end where items enter and leave</td><td>the plate on top of the pile</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>push(x)</code></td><td>add x on the top</td><td>put a clean plate on the pile</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>pop()</code></td><td>remove the top item and return it</td><td>take the top plate</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>peek()</code></td><td>return the top item, but keep it there</td><td>look at the top plate without taking it</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>size()</code></td><td>how many items the stack holds</td><td>count the plates</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>isEmpty()</code></td><td><code>true</code> when there are no items</td><td>no plates left</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>isFull()</code></td><td><code>true</code> when a fixed-size stack has no room</td><td>the pile reaches the shelf above</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>These names are conventions: ordinary method names that a programmer writes, not Java keywords. A different class could call them something else.</p>
      <div class="def">
        <p class="note-label">Underflow and overflow</p>
        <p><b>Underflow</b>: popping or peeking when the stack is <b>empty</b> (no plates left). <b>Overflow</b>: pushing onto a fixed-size stack that is already <b>full</b> (the shelf has no room).</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={stkFlowPic()} /><figcaption>Both are mistakes in <i>when</i> the program calls a method, not problems with the data. In Part 4, each method checks for them first. Overflow can only happen when the storage has a fixed size, like an array.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Where you see LIFO</h3>
        <p>Here are three everyday places where the newest item comes out first:</p>
        <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={stkLifoPic()} /><figcaption>In each one, the orange item went in last, so it is the first to come out.</figcaption></figure>
        <p>Here is the full list, with two you will meet in later lessons:</p>
        <div class="scroll-x">
          <table class="tbl">
            <thead><tr><th>Use</th><th>How LIFO fits</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Undo</td><td>Each change could be pushed. Undo pops the most recent change first.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>The Back button</td><td>Each page you visit could be pushed. Back pops the most recent page.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Reversing a word</td><td>Push the letters, then pop them all: they come out in reverse (MATI becomes ITAM).</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Method calls in Java</td><td>Java keeps a stack of calls. A call is pushed when a method starts and popped when it finishes. You will see this in the <a href={base + '/itc120/recursion/'}>Recursion</a> lesson.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Depth-first search</td><td>A stack remembers the path back. You will use it in the <a href={base + '/itc120/dfs/'}>DFS</a> lesson.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="small muted">Undo and Back are illustrations of the rule, not a description of how any particular app is programmed.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="lab">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 2 · Try it</p>
      <h2>Stack lab</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Push three values, then pop them. In which order do they come out? Then try <b>peek</b>: what changes, and what does not?</p>
      <SQLab id="lab-stack" mode="stack" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="trace">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 3 · Tracing</p>
      <h2>Tracing a stack in a table</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>A <b>trace table</b> shows the stack after every call. This is also the format for exams and coding exercises: one row per call, the stack written <b>bottom → top</b> with commas, and the value the call returns (— if it returns nothing). The stack starts empty, and each row acts on the result of the row before it:</p>
      <div class="scroll-x">
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>Call</th><th>Stack after (bottom → top)</th><th>Returned</th><th>size</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>push(10)</code></td><td>10</td><td>—</td><td>1</td><td>10 goes on; it is the top.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>push(20)</code></td><td>10, 20</td><td>—</td><td>2</td><td>20 goes above 10; 20 is the top.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>peek()</code></td><td>10, 20</td><td><b>20</b></td><td>2</td><td>Reads the top. Nothing changes.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>push(30)</code></td><td>10, 20, 30</td><td>—</td><td>3</td><td>30 is the new top.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>pop()</code></td><td>10, 20</td><td><b>30</b></td><td>2</td><td>Removes 30; 20 is the top again.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>pop()</code></td><td>10</td><td><b>20</b></td><td>1</td><td>Removes 20; only 10 is left.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="trap">
        <p class="note-label">Exam traps</p>
        <ol class="flow">
          <li><b>Write the stack bottom → top.</b> The newest item is written <b>last</b> (on the right), not first. So "10, 20, 30" means 30 is on top, like D in <Peek label="the A, B, C, D picture" title="From Part 1" html={defSvg} />.</li>
          <li><b>A call removes even when nothing prints it.</b> A line like <code>s.pop();</code> still removes the top item. Its value is returned and simply not used, so write it in the Returned column anyway.</li>
          <li><b><code>peek()</code> changes nothing.</b> Copy the stack from the row above, and write the top value as Returned.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="java">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 4 · Java</p>
      <h2>A stack in Java, using an array</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <div class="def">
        <p class="note-label">Array and index (from the Arrays lesson)</p>
        <p>An <b>array</b> is a fixed number of cells side by side, all holding the same type. Each cell has an <b>index</b>, its position number, starting at 0. An array's <b>length</b> never changes, so the last index is <code>length - 1</code>.</p>
      </div>
      <p>The class <code>ArrayStack</code> keeps its items in an array called <code>data</code>. The field <code>top</code> is the <b>index of the top item</b>. A new stack has <code>top = -1</code>, meaning "no top item yet." The -1 is only an index; it is never returned as data. Why not start at 0? <code>top = 0</code> would mean "the top item is in cell 0," so the stack would already hold one item.</p>
      <pre class="code"><code set:html={hiN(stackFields)} /></pre>
      <div class="def">
        <p class="note-label">Boolean method</p>
        <p>A <b>boolean method</b> returns <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>. <code>return top == -1;</code> returns <code>true</code> exactly when <code>top</code> equals -1.</p>
      </div>
      <p>The three checks all come from the meaning of <code>top</code>:</p>
      <div class="scroll-x">
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Returns</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>isEmpty()</code></td><td><code>top == -1</code></td><td>no top item yet</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>isFull()</code></td><td><code>top == data.length - 1</code></td><td>the top item is in the last cell</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>size()</code></td><td><code>top + 1</code></td><td>indexes start at 0, so top 1 means 2 items</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="def">
        <p class="note-label">Exception and <code>throw</code></p>
        <p>An <b>exception</b> is Java's way of stopping a method when it cannot do its job. The <code>throw</code> statement stops the method at once with an exception object. <code>IllegalStateException</code> is Java's standard exception for "you asked at the wrong time," such as popping an empty stack. If nothing in the program handles it, the program stops, and Java prints the exception's name and message.</p>
      </div>
      <p>Now <code>push</code>, <code>pop</code>, and <code>peek</code>. Each one first checks for overflow or underflow, then does its job. Follow them on a new stack made with <code>new ArrayStack(3)</code>. The array is drawn as a column, with index 0 at the bottom, so the top is really on top:</p>
      <CodeWalk id="cw-stack" code={stackWalkCode} steps={stackWalkSteps()} cap="Walkthrough: push(4), push(9), pop(), peek(), then overflow" hint="Use Next to go step by step, or tap a line to jump to it." />
      <div class="remember">
        <p class="note-label">What each method changes</p>
        <p><code>push</code> moves <code>top</code> up one cell, then stores the value there. <code>pop</code> saves the value at <code>top</code>, moves <code>top</code> down, and returns the saved value. <code>peek</code> changes <b>nothing</b>: it returns <code>data[top]</code> and leaves <code>top</code> alone.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="plain">
        <p class="note-label">Why not return -1 when the stack is empty?</p>
        <p>-1 could be real data: a stack of temperatures might hold -1. Throwing an exception makes the mistake impossible to miss. The rule: check <code>isEmpty()</code> before popping or peeking, and <code>isFull()</code> before pushing onto a fixed-size array.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>The whole program</h3>
        <p>Here is everything in one runnable file. <code>main</code> does the same calls as the walkthrough, except the last <code>push(8)</code>. To see the overflow exception, add <code>s.push(8);</code> at the end of <code>main</code>.</p>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hiN(fullProgram)} /></pre>
        <TryIt />
      </div>
      <div class="plain">
        <p class="note-label">Good to know: Java's ready-made stacks</p>
        <p>Java already has stack classes: <code>java.util.Stack</code> and the newer <code>ArrayDeque</code>, which Java's documentation recommends. Both have <code>push</code>, <code>pop</code>, and <code>peek</code>. In this course you build your own, so you can see what happens inside.</p>
        <p>You may also meet <code>StackOverflowError</code>. That is overflow on Java's own stack of method calls, usually from a recursive method that never stops. You will see it in the <a href={base + '/itc120/recursion/'}>Recursion</a> lesson.</p>
      </div>
      <details class="reveal">
        <summary>Optional: a stack built from a linked list</summary>
        <div class="flow">
          <p>A stack can also use a <a href={base + '/itc120/linked-lists/'}>linked list</a>, with the <b>first node as the top</b>. Then <code>push</code> is "insert at the beginning" and <code>pop</code> is "delete the first node," the same two operations from the Linked lists lesson. It never gets full, so it has no <code>isFull()</code>.</p>
          <pre class="code"><code set:html={hiN(linkedStack)} /></pre>
          <p class="small muted">It uses the same <code>Node</code> class as the Linked lists lesson. LIFO is the rule; an array or a linked list is just where the items are kept.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="mistakes">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 5 · Watch out</p>
      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <div class="trap">
        <p class="note-label">Mistake 1: storing before moving <code>top</code></p>
        <p><b>Situation:</b> a student swaps the two lines of <code>push</code>:</p>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(wrongPush)} /></pre>
        <p><b>What goes wrong:</b> <code>top</code> is the index of the current top item. On an empty stack, <code>data[-1] = value;</code> crashes with an <code>ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</code>. On a stack holding 4, 9, the new value writes over the 9.</p>
        <p><b>Fix:</b> move first (<code>top++;</code>), then store (<code>data[top] = value;</code>).</p>
      </div>
      <div class="trap">
        <p class="note-label">Mistake 2: thinking <code>pop</code> erases the cell</p>
        <p><b>Situation:</b> after <code>pop()</code> on 4, 9, a student writes that <code>data[1]</code> is now 0.</p>
        <p><b>What goes wrong:</b> <code>pop</code> only moves <code>top</code> down. The 9 is <b>still in cell 1</b> (see step 9 of the walkthrough). It is not part of the stack because it is above <code>top</code>, and the next <code>push</code> writes over it.</p>
        <div class="mk-ans mk-solo">
          <p class="mk-wrong"><b>✗ After pop()</b><span class="mono">data = [4, 0, 0]</span></p>
          <p class="mk-right"><b>✓ After pop()</b><span class="mono">data = [4, 9, 0], top = 0</span></p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="trap">
        <p class="note-label">Mistake 3: using <code>peek</code> to remove</p>
        <p><b>Situation:</b> a program calls <code>peek()</code> in a loop, hoping to empty the stack.</p>
        <p><b>What goes wrong:</b> <code>peek()</code> never moves <code>top</code>, so it returns the same top item every time and the stack never shrinks.</p>
        <p><b>Fix:</b> use <code>pop()</code> to remove; use <code>peek()</code> only to look.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="practice">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 6 · Your turn</p>
      <h2>Practice on paper</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Set A is the Section 5 self-check from your module, in case you want to redo it. Set B is new.</p>
      <div class="paper flow">
        <p class="note-label">Set A: from the module</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Write exactly what this prints.<pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(s1)} /></pre></li>
          <li>In your own words, what is the difference between <code>pop()</code> and <code>peek()</code>? Which line of code shows it?</li>
        </ol>
        <details class="reveal"><summary>Show the answers</summary><div class="flow">
          <ol>
